\DOC LOCAL_EPADD_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_EPADD_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually} machine-state goal across a called subroutine block,
replacing the current state variable by its successor and adding an assumption
specifying the subroutine's effect.

\DESCRIBE
The single argument is an {int} giving the index {n} of the current state, i.e.
the numeric suffix of the state variable {sn} occurring in the goal's
assumptions and conclusion; the tactic forms the next state by incrementing
that index to {n+1}. The conclusion retains the same {eventually} postcondition
but is asserted of {s(n+1)} rather than {sn}. Assumptions mentioning the old
state are discarded and re-asserted about the new state, with these exceptions:
the flag-reading assumptions are dropped; the program-counter assumption is
replaced by one giving a new code address; and the accumulated "may change"
frame assumption is re-stated for the new state with extra components appended.
One further assumption is introduced, universally quantified, relating the
inputs of the called block to the memory contents it writes in the new state.
Assumptions not mentioning the state variable are left in place.
{
   A ?- t
   =============================  LOCAL_EPADD_TAC n
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where {A'} and {t'} are {A} and {t} with the state variable advanced
as described, and {A'} additionally contains the new universally quantified
subroutine-effect assumption {!z1 ... zp. ...}.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this same single-subgoal transformation on goals of
this {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
